

**18. Weierstrass Elliptic and Related Functions**

**Mathematical Properties**

**18.1. Definitions, Symbolism, Restrictions and Conventions**

An elliptic function is a single-valued doubly periodic function of a single complex variable which is analytic except at poles and whose only singularities in the finite plane are poles. If \(\omega\) and \(\omega^{\prime}\) are a pair of (primitive) half-periods of such a function \(f(z)\), then \(f(z{+}2M\omega{+}2N\omega^{\prime}){=}f(z)\), \(M\) and \(N\) being integers. Thus the study of any such function can be reduced to consideration of its behavior in a _fundamental period parallelogram_ (FPP). An elliptic function has a finite number of poles (and the same number of zeros) in a FPP; the number of such poles (zeros) (an irreducible set) is the _order_ of the function (poles and zeros are counted according to their multiplicity). All other poles (zeros) are called _congruent_ to the irreducible set. The simplest (non-trivial) elliptic functions are of order two. One may choose as the standard function of order two either a function with two simple poles (Jacobi's choice) or one double pole (Weierstrass' choice) in a FPP.

_Weierstrass' \(\mathcal{O}\)-Function._ Let \(\omega\), \(\omega^{\prime}\) denote a pair of complex numbers with \(\mathcal{I}(\omega^{\prime}/\omega){>}0\). Then \(\mathcal{O}(z){=}\mathcal{O}(z|\omega)\) is an elliptic function of order two with periods \(2\omega\), \(2\omega^{\prime}\) and having a double pole at \(z{=}0\), whose principal part is \(z^{-2}\); \(\mathcal{O}(z){-}z^{-2}\) is analytic in a neighborhood of the origin and vanishes at \(z{=}0\).

_Weierstrass' \(\mathcal{I}\)-Function \(\xi(z){=}\xi(z|\omega\), \(\omega^{\prime})\)_ satisfies the condition \(\xi^{\prime}(z){=}\mathcal{O}(z)\); further, \(\xi(z)\) has a simple pole at \(z{=}0\) whose principal part is \(z^{-1}\); \(\xi(z){-}z^{-1}\) vanishes at \(z{=}0\) and is analytic in a neighborhood of the origin. \(\xi(z)\) is _NOT_ an elliptic function, since it is not periodic. However, it is quasi-periodic (see "period" relations), so reduction to FPP is possible.

_Weierstrass' \(\sigma\)-Function \(\sigma(z){=}\sigma(z|\omega\), \(\omega^{\prime})\)_ satisfies the condition \(\sigma^{\prime}(z)/\sigma(z){=}\xi(z)\); further, \(\sigma(z)\) is an entire function which vanishes at the origin. Take \(\xi\), it is _NOT_ an elliptic function, since it is not periodic. However, it is quasi-periodic (see "period" relations), so reduction to FPP is possible.

_Weierstrass' \(\sigma\)-Function \(\sigma(z){=}\sigma(z|\omega\), \(\omega^{\prime})\)_ satisfies the condition \(\sigma^{\prime}(z)/\sigma(z){=}\xi(z)\); further, \(\sigma(z)\) is an entire function which vanishes at the origin. Take \(\xi\), it is _NOT_ an elliptic function, since it is not periodic. However, it is quasi-periodic (see "period" relations), so reduction to FPP is possible.

